1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,493
Every command works with three streams: input, normal output, and error output.

2
00:00:05,513 --> 00:00:08,081
Redirection decides where each one goes.

3
00:00:08,551 --> 00:00:09,556
So in this video:

4
00:00:09,576 --> 00:00:14,135
use input-output redirection, with greater-than, double greater-than, pipes,

5
00:00:14,155 --> 00:00:15,401
and two greater-than.

6
00:00:19,421 --> 00:00:22,004
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

7
00:00:22,474 --> 00:00:24,234
Use input-output redirection:

8
00:00:24,254 --> 00:00:28,470
greater-than, double greater-than, pipe, two greater-than, and so on.

9
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:32,838
We'll send output to files, append to them, chain commands together,

10
00:00:32,858 --> 00:00:35,085
and separate errors from normal output.

11
00:00:42,605 --> 00:00:48,055
Three streams matter. Standard input is stream zero. Standard output is stream one.

12
00:00:48,075 --> 00:00:49,966
Standard error is stream two.

13
00:01:01,436 --> 00:01:04,056
By default, input comes from the keyboard,

14
00:01:04,076 --> 00:01:08,456
and both output and errors go to the screen. Redirection changes that.

15
00:01:17,976 --> 00:01:23,461
We start with one small command at a time. M K D I R means make directory.

16
00:01:23,481 --> 00:01:26,511
We make a folder called redir to work in.

17
00:01:29,481 --> 00:01:34,687
E C H O prints text on the screen. That's all it does. Here it prints first line.

18
00:01:37,657 --> 00:01:42,804
Now the greater-than sign. It sends the output to a file instead of the screen.

19
00:01:42,824 --> 00:01:46,597
Nothing appears, because the text went into out dot t x t.

20
00:01:49,567 --> 00:01:53,067
C A T shows what is inside a file. There is our first line.

21
00:01:56,037 --> 00:02:00,391
Run it again with new text. Greater-than replaces whatever the file held.

22
00:02:03,361 --> 00:02:07,458
Only the second line is left. The first one is gone. That is overwrite.

23
00:02:11,478 --> 00:02:13,490
Double greater-than appends instead.

24
00:02:13,510 --> 00:02:17,047
It adds to the end of the file and keeps what is already there.

25
00:02:20,017 --> 00:02:22,749
Now the file holds the second line, then the third.

26
00:02:26,769 --> 00:02:27,867
A pipe is the vertical bar.

27
00:02:27,887 --> 00:02:31,055
It sends the output of the command on its left into the command on its right.

28
00:02:31,075 --> 00:02:32,422
L S lists the files in slash etc.

29
00:02:32,442 --> 00:02:35,154
Head shows only the first lines, and dash n five means five lines.

30
00:02:44,624 --> 00:02:48,046
Here the right side is W C dash L. W C counts, and dash L counts lines.

31
00:02:48,066 --> 00:02:51,196
So slash etc has one hundred eighty-three entries on this system.

32
00:02:58,716 --> 00:03:02,411
Now errors. L S is asked for two paths: one that does not exist, and one that does.

33
00:03:02,431 --> 00:03:05,992
We get an error message and a normal result, both on the screen, mixed together.

34
00:03:14,462 --> 00:03:16,995
Two greater-than sends only the errors to a file.

35
00:03:17,015 --> 00:03:19,705
Greater-than good dot t x t takes the normal output.

36
00:03:19,725 --> 00:03:23,978
Two greater-than bad dot t x t takes the errors. Nothing prints on the screen now.

37
00:03:26,948 --> 00:03:29,829
Good dot t x t holds the normal result.

38
00:03:32,799 --> 00:03:37,835
And bad dot t x t holds the error message. The two streams are now separate.

39
00:03:41,855 --> 00:03:45,611
To put both streams in one file, redirect the output first,

40
00:03:45,631 --> 00:03:49,067
then add two greater-than, ampersand, one. That means:

41
00:03:49,087 --> 00:03:51,627
send errors to the same place as output.

42
00:03:59,097 --> 00:04:07,738
Both lines are in both dot t x t.

43
00:04:10,708 --> 00:04:15,931
To throw output away, send it to slash dev slash null. Think of it as a black hole.

44
00:04:15,951 --> 00:04:18,710
The error disappears and nothing is printed.

45
00:04:22,730 --> 00:04:26,281
Less-than feeds a file to a command as its input.

46
00:04:26,301 --> 00:04:30,070
W C dash L reads out dot t x t and counts two lines.

47
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:37,895
Tee writes to a file and also shows the output on screen.

48
00:04:37,915 --> 00:04:40,811
Here it saves saved and shown into tee dot t x t, and we still see it.

49
00:04:43,781 --> 00:04:46,427
And cat confirms the file holds the same text.

50
00:04:50,447 --> 00:04:53,883
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

51
00:04:54,353 --> 00:04:57,113
Files live on disk, so they survive a reboot.

52
00:04:57,133 --> 00:04:59,645
We restart the lab, and it comes back up.

53
00:05:03,115 --> 00:05:06,411
Uptime says zero minutes, so this really is a fresh boot.

54
00:05:06,431 --> 00:05:09,495
The checker later confirms the files are still there.

55
00:05:15,015 --> 00:05:18,534
Your turn. Create a directory called lab3.

56
00:05:18,554 --> 00:05:23,422
Run L S on slash etc slash hostname and slash nonexistent,

57
00:05:23,442 --> 00:05:30,163
so that normal output goes to ok dot t x t, and the error goes to err dot t x t.

58
00:05:33,133 --> 00:05:38,340
Both files go in lab3. Then run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

59
00:05:44,360 --> 00:05:48,116
Next up: Use grep and regular expressions to analyze text.

60
00:05:48,586 --> 00:05:51,489
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
